- Windows Task Manager now shows fleetd as "Fleet" instead of "Orbit osquery runtime and autoupdater". App Control for Business (WDAC) rules that match orbit.exe by file description need the new name; the product name stays "Fleet osquery".
- **Breaking change:** Once the Fleet server is on Fleet 5, fleetd renames its Windows Installed apps entry from "Fleet osquery" to "Fleet", and its Linux process from `orbit` to `Fleet`. Service names, the launchd label, the systemd unit, package names, the install directory and the installer name don't change. Update policies, scripts and monitoring that look for the name "Fleet osquery" on Windows or the process name `orbit` on Linux.
- **Breaking change:** Once the Fleet server is on Fleet 5, fleetd on macOS runs from `/opt/orbit/bin/orbit/macos/<channel>/Fleet`, started through `/opt/orbit/bin/orbit/Fleet`, so Activity Monitor, `ps` and `top` show `Fleet`. It moves only when a Full Disk Access profile allows the new path, or when it has no Full Disk Access to lose, and moves back to the `orbit` path when a profile only allows that one. Add the new paths to Full Disk Access profiles before upgrading, and update EDR rules and scripts that match the `orbit` path or process name.
